## Findings

**Fundamental view remains Medium confidence.** CP-1D strengthens the need to
test earnings definitions and cash allocation; it does not establish that all
issuer adjustments are invalid or that the issuer lacks debt-service capacity.
Annual ordinary EBITDA 3,813 and issuer EBITDA As Defined 4,173 both reconcile
to reported data. The adjustments include recurring stock/deferred compensation
and repeated acquisition/refinancing categories. Other is heterogeneous and
changes sign, including a negative quarterly adjustment. Accordingly, no
resolved quality-adjusted earnings point or sustainable FCF estimate is supplied.

The creditor mechanism is specific: if demand/margins weaken while compensation,
integration and financing costs persist, removing them from an earnings measure
can overstate the resources available after cash interest, taxes and required
investment. Annual OCF conversion of 53.63% on ordinary EBITDA versus 49.01% on
issuer As Defined is a mechanical definition comparison. The source cash bridge
contains the actual interest/tax/working-capital effects; those are not deducted
again or attributed entirely to unsupported add-backs.

The strongest counterargument is that the issuer reports positive earnings/OCF,
reconciles its definitions and achieved higher quarterly operating cash and
earnings. Its current quarterly As Defined metric is below ordinary EBITDA,
which directly contradicts a generic claim that all adjustments inflate results.
This evidence supports the business's observed ability to generate cash, while
leaving recurrence, future conversion and creditor access uncertain.

CP-1E's stated convention adds operating lease liability 62 and gross recognised
pension liabilities 19 to the September carrying-debt base 24,880, producing a
known-item subtotal 24,961. Finance leases, securitization and government advances
already counted in that base are not added again. The separate pension asset 13
and net funded deficit 6 are different measures; unrestricted plan-asset netting
was not assumed. Pension liability is not an immediate cash funding demand.
This changes the mechanical annual As Defined leverage comparison from 5.9621x
to 5.9815x; it neither quantifies all claims nor establishes agency/covenant debt.

The material cash-allocation issue remains the declared distribution and the
observed subsequent cash bridge, rather than the small quantified lease/pension
increment alone. Dividends payable 4,216 are a separate committed shareholder
cash use under this convention; they cannot be ignored in liquidity reasoning.
The quarterly 4,396 dividend/equivalent cash outflow is not the single October
event, and the annual/interim 132/131 equivalents difference remains unresolved.
Reported December cash 2,459 and the issuer's cash-liquidity measure do not
provide current unrestricted-cash or forward sufficiency evidence.

The causal downside remains demand/cost pressure → weaker cash conversion while
recurring obligations persist → less buffer after cash allocation → greater
facility/refinancing dependence. Conversion discipline, realised integration
outcomes, restrained distributions and dated accessible liquidity would
strengthen the creditor case; further extraction or deteriorating conversion
would weaken it. Monitor signed adjustment composition/recurrence, actual cash
settlements, acquisition outcomes, distributions and facility renewal milestones.
No rating, covenant breach, default probability or current refinancing outcome
is inferred.

